The Vocabulary of Interlinked Datasets (VoID)
---------------------------------------------

src/void/schema/void.yaml, src/void/datamodel/, project/ and the examples
under tests/data/ model the Vocabulary of Interlinked Datasets (VoID),
whose terms are named in the namespace http://rdfs.org/ns/void#. Keith
Alexander, Richard Cyganiak, Michael Hausenblas and Jun Zhao are its
authors. They explain it in "Describing Linked Datasets with the VoID
Vocabulary", a W3C Interest Group Note of 3 March 2011:
https://www.w3.org/TR/2011/NOTE-void-20110303/. The vocabulary's own
file, which declares its classes and properties, is kept by its editors
at https://github.com/cygri/void/blob/master/rdfs/void.ttl. This
project's schema is its own work. It is not W3C's or the editors', and
its use does not imply endorsement by either.

The Note is Copyright (c) 2010 W3C (MIT, ERCIM, Keio). Its page says that
W3C's document use rules apply and links to the W3C Document License
(https://www.w3.org/Consortium/Legal/copyright-documents). That licence
lets anyone copy and share the Note unchanged, with its notice. It allows
a changed version only in software and its documentation, with a notice,
and never as a specification. The vocabulary file states no licence, and
the editors' repository has none. Therefore, this repository copies
neither: no definition, comment, label or example from the Note or the
file, copied or paraphrased.

What the repository carries is fact and this project's own words. The
schema names each VoID class and property by its IRI and gives it the
domain, range and cardinality the vocabulary file and the Note state. Every
description is this project's, written from what the term is for, not by
rewording the Note's sentence. tests/upstream/void-terms.tsv lists the
IRIs the vocabulary file declares, with the domain, range, parent and
functional facts it states for each, and nothing else. The file was read
at the editors' commit 5b2ca68b7897 and is pinned by its git object hash.
An IRI is an identifier; naming one is what a vocabulary is for.

The schema also names terms of the DCMI Metadata Terms
(http://purl.org/dc/terms/), FOAF (http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/), RDF
Schema, the SPARQL 1.1 Service Description vocabulary and DCAT by their
IRIs, as the Note does. tests/upstream/vocabulary-terms.txt lists the IRIs
of those vocabularies' terms, read from each vocabulary's published file,
which is pinned by its checksum. No text of theirs is copied.

